New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Chair, for convening today’s discussion on Chad.
Thanks also to Deputy Minister of Finance, Budget, Economy, and Planning Fatima Haram Acyl, for being with us today and for her valuable briefing.
I further thank Assistant Secretary-General Pobee and today’s other panelists for their presentations.
The United States is supportive of peace and stability efforts in Chad. National reconciliation must continue to be central to peacebuilding efforts as a foundation for peace and stability.
The Chad National Development Plan “Tchad Connexion 2030” underscores the centrality and cross-cutting nature of state reforms, decentralization, peace, security, and diplomacy.
The United States welcomes the establishment of the first bicameral parliament in Chad’s history earlier this year.
The National Seminar on Decentralization in Chad, launched by President Deby on July 21, is an important next step in the decentralization initiative, which helpfully highlights needed cooperation and coordination with the provincial and local councils.
In closing, today’s discussion serves as an important reminder of the importance of peacebuilding efforts that are integrated, strategic, and coherent, grounded in supporting national strategies and efforts. Thank you.
