Ambassador Mike Waltz
U.S. Representative
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
LARRY KUDLOW: We bring on Mr. Mike Waltz, who is now the U.S. Ambassador to the United Nations. Mike – Ambassador Mike. Thank you very much. So, let me get this right. Trump speaks yesterday, okay? It looked like UN was sabotaging him. The escalator didn’t work. By the way, that could have been very dangerous for him, Melania. The teleprompter didn’t work. Yes, he can ad lib, but then we find out that there was no sound in the auditorium.
AMBASSADOR MIKE WALTZ: And to add one more insult to injury here, was his speech, in the middle of it, switched to Portuguese in terms of the broadcast, and then, and then back again. Look, the whole thing is unacceptable. The whole thing stinks. There’s 150 world leaders there, and this only happens to him. Not once, not twice, but three times.
KUDLOW: Do you think it was deliberate?
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: It’s well – look, it’s under investigation. I’ll tell you, just in fairness, the Secretary-General, who I’ve talked to twice now, has opened their doors. Come see the video. We’ll fully cooperate. We’ll get to the bottom of it, and all those instances, and secret services on the case. So, a lot of folks don’t realize that’s its own mini little like Vatican City there, and as the Ambassador I said, you got to open your doors. And some people were kind of shoulder shrugging this. This could have been incredibly serious. Right? It’s insulting, and it’s right here on American soil.
KUDLOW: His message was so phenomenal. I mean, he just slammed them, he buried them. One, two, three, they’re all sitting there like this, and there’s no sound. Come on.
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: Energy and immigration. Calling – There are seven UN agencies focused on climate. Regardless of what you think, the whole thing, the whole bureaucracy needs to be cut back.
KUDLOW: How much do we cut that? How much do we cut the UN by?
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: Well, we withheld this year.
KUDLOW: Fair amount? Paid a good amount?
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: Yeah, we haven’t paid any of it. And my first meeting with Secretary-General was, here are the reforms that we need to see before you start talking about taxpayer dollars.
KUDLOW: Oh, I love it. We haven’t had a really good reform ambassador in a long time.
AMBASSADOR WALTZ: The last time was 1999, Senator Jesse Helms said, we’re going to clean house before you get another American dollar. And we have every obligation to make sure it’s transparent. Do you think cleaning up the federal government was tough? How about this 193 globalist, multinational thing – and my wife says I’m a masochist.
KUDLOW: And I know you, and you’re a bulldog, and you’re going to do it. Ambassador Mike Waltz. Love it. Congratulations, by the way.
