AS DELIVERED
As we observe the 80th anniversary of the UN Charter, this is a moment to reaffirm our commitment to ensuring the UN is both effective and efficient in delivering on its core mandates.
The United States generally supports the measures aimed at streamlining the General Assembly’s work and reducing overlaps and redundancies.
That includes, for example, efforts by the General Committee to harmonize practices in the Committees’ work with a view to supporting timely and responsive Committee, and ultimately Assembly, decisions, reducing the number of meetings, streamlining agenda items, and leveraging technology for efficiency.
There are a number of agenda items that will be taken up in the 80th session, including those under sub-heading A on “Promotion of sustained economic growth and sustainable development in accordance with the relevant resolutions of the General Assembly and recent United Nations conferences,” that unhelpfully distract from the UN’s ability to focus on promoting international peace and security.
At the same time, we should avoid efforts to expand the role of the Assembly in a manner that is not consistent with the UN Charter.
Per GA revitalization resolution 79/327 adopted on September 5, and in the spirit of UN80, we look forward to rigorously reviewing the Assembly’s working methods during the 80th session and submitting concrete proposals to the General Committee.
The United States will support measures that streamline the Assembly’s work, reduce the number of meetings, and leverage technology for efficiency.
Colleagues, we note that six new agenda items have been proposed this year by Member States for the 80th session.
Inclusion of new items on the agenda is without prejudice to any positions that the United States will take on the substantive items, requests and resolutions.
We look forward to carefully considering these new observer requests substantively during the Sixth Committee’s deliberations.
