Jonathan Shrier
Acting Representative of the United States on the Economic and Social Council of the United Nations
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
This Annual Session of the Executive Board comes at a pivotal time for the reform of the UN and the UN Development System. As we look to the coming months with UN80 and the next round of UN Funds and Programs’ Strategic Plans on the horizon, the United Nations and indeed, UN Women, must focus on delivering where it matters most – for individuals and communities.
The United States expects UN Women– and all UN entities – to achieve greater efficiency and transparency, eliminate duplication, consolidate functions, and return to core mandates. These considerations are vital in the implementation of UN Women’s next Strategic Plan, which should reflect the spirit of reform in UN80 as you respond to today’s pressing global challenges.
The United States support efforts to implement policies aimed at protecting women, combatting violence, and enhancing women’s representation and leadership. This also means to acknowledge biological truth.
We expect UN Women to ensure that its programming and documents use terminology that clearly reflects the biological reality there are two sexes – male and female. A person’s sex is unchangeable. UN Women should reconsider the use of the term “gender” in all applicable policies and documents.
In addition, we also strongly encourage the United Nations to avoid using diversity, equity, and inclusion concepts and terminology in documents and programming.
While the youngest UN entity, UN Women is the lead UN entity in ensuring equality between women and men throughout the UN system. UN Women’s efforts must defend women’s unalienable rights and protect biological truth by using clear and accurate language and policies that recognize women are biologically female, and men are biologically male. Any use of radical gender ideology is an attack on women and the Trump Administration is reclaiming biological reality and truth.
The Trump Administration is committed to supporting families, promoting women’s health, and protecting children at all stages of life.
There has unequivocally never been an international commitment on “sexual and reproductive health and rights,” and we strongly oppose any reference to this non-agreed term. The United States reaffirms that there is no international right to abortion.
Its integrated triple mandate – normative support, United Nations system coordination, and operational activities – enables UN Women to serve an essential role in striving to ensure that every woman and girl has what she needs to live up to her full potential.
