AS DELIVERED
Mr. President,
The United States has an objection and wishes to request a vote on the decision to adopt a Country Program Document for China.
The United States will not support initiatives that do not align with U.S. priorities. As an Executive Board member, the United States takes seriously its oversight responsibility over agency operations and how they allocate their resources, including for the UNICEF Country Program Document for China for 2026-2030.
China is the world’s second largest economy. A country that can fund its own space program and build aircraft carriers should fund its own development. Every UNICEF dollar spent in China is one less for countries that truly depend on that funding. Letting China draw on UNICEF resources risks subsidizing one country’s geopolitical agenda as it promotes national initiatives like the Global Development Initiative under a multilateral umbrella. The UN was formed to support the vulnerable, not underwrite geopolitical ambitions of countries. This is why we are voting against this Country Program Document.
We know that China will be watching how each country votes, and as a result, its threat to retaliate will deter countries from taking a stance on countering this situation. We are not afraid to stand up or speak up and we are not afraid to state that scarce resources should not be diverted to member states that can manage their own development priorities.
China’s history with the One-Child policy and forced sterilizations warrants our refusal to endorse a UNICEF Country Program Document. Devoting precious UNICEF resources to China makes a mockery of this process, and we regret that the many members of the board who espouse their support for children will not take a public stance against this document.
Moving forward, we urge the Executive Board to ensure justifiable criteria serve as the basis for resource allocation by UNICEF.
The United States also strongly objects to the UN’s promotion of any member state’s foreign or development policy, including the Global Development Initiative.
Mr. President, thank you for the opportunity to request a vote and deliver this explanation of vote.
