Jonathan Shrier
Acting ECOSOC Representative
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President, and, thank you also to the distinguished Permanent Representative of Germany, for convening today’s meeting. We would like to express our appreciation to Assistant Secretary-General for Peacebuilding Elizabeth Spehar for the related 2024 Peacebuilding Commission (PBC) and Peacebuilding Fund (PBF) Reports.
The United Nations remains important to resolving complex international challenges and threats to stability, including on conflict prevention; however, it has drifted from its core mission of peacemaking.
The United Nations must return to its principal purposes, notably maintaining international peace and security and addressing the causes of armed conflict.
Peacebuilding must be led and owned by the affected country, with the UN providing support tailored to local needs and priorities.
The Peacebuilding Commission can serve as a key intergovernmental advisory body, providing strategic guidance and mobilizing resources for peacebuilding initiatives.
At the same time, Peacebuilding Fund resources should support conflict prevention and resolution efforts, including cross-border and regional approaches and facilitating UN transitions.
While there is now a modest allocation of assessed funding for the PBF as part of the UN Regular Budget, we stress that voluntary contributions should remain the primary source of PBF funding.
In order to be effective, UN peacebuilding must ensure outcomes that bring meaningful and material conflict prevention and lasting peace in meeting the needs of its many stakeholders.
International donors alone cannot prevent conflict. Without the buy-in of parties to the conflict, UN peacebuilding will not be effective.
We encourage the Peacebuilding Support Office to continue efforts to highlight specific results and frame evidence in terms of outcomes. These measures aid in retaining member state support and investment.
The ongoing Peacebuilding Architecture Review presents an important opportunity to improve UN peacebuilding efforts through enhanced coherence on implementation within the UN system, strengthened cooperation with international financial institutions, and improved integration of human rights.
Additionally, the Architecture Review must continue to center on national ownership in conflict prevention and peacebuilding efforts, including through national prevention strategies.
Thank you.
