Thank you, Mr. President.
Thank you for your remarks, Executive Director Russell.
The United States recognizes children are the least likely to start a conflict but most likely to suffer. That is why the United States is committed to providing life-saving humanitarian assistance and effective, strategic, efficient, and time-bound responses to disasters. We recognize the enormous role that UNICEF plays in safeguarding the health, safety and well-being of children in the most trying circumstances and thank the dedicated staff who stay and deliver, frequently in the most difficult of circumstances. We are distressed by reports that armed Houthis captured UNICEF staff in Yemen just days ago; humanitarian workers should not be a target.
In addition, we recognize UNICEF’s leadership in risk assessment and risk management. Other organizations should adopt UNICEF’s comprehensive approach to the evaluation and management of risk.
However, the United States can no longer shoulder the majority of humanitarian assistance globally. Burden-sharing is essential, and it has never been more important for UNICEF and the other funds and programs to pursue greater innovation in financing, including expanded partnerships with the private sector and alternative funding models to enhance program sustainability.
UNICEF must focus on its core mandate and avoid distracting narratives that fall outside that mission – like the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and ambiguous gender language. The focus must remain on tailored, high-impact support for interventions such as primary health care, access to safe water and sanitation, and nutrition, to address preventable child mortality and disability. Working across the UN Country Team under the leadership of the Resident Coordinator allows UNICEF to firmly focus on its core competitive advantage addressing the needs and wellbeing of children.
We cannot lose sight of our shared goals to ensure every child not only survives but thrives. The United States believes that mothers and fathers know how to best raise their children and create a thriving society. We firmly believe that harmful gender ideology is detrimental to the well-being and development of children, and we remain concerned that UNICEF is promoting an ideological agenda under the guise of sexual and reproductive health and rights.
In conclusion, we must move away from protracted aid toward peaceful, lasting solutions to conflict and crisis, expanded trade, and local resource mobilization. We look forward to working with UNICEF and other member states toward that end.
